  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Countertrade Products obligated $244M in FY2026 to date on 747 awards, with an average award size of $326K. Obligations fell 50.5% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations peaked at $492M in FY25 and then moved to $244M in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $106M, followed by the Department of the Interior with $39.3M and the Department of Homeland Security with $20.3M. Other notable buyers include the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $16.4M and the Department of Justice at $12.1M. Other Computer Related Services is the largest market at $204M. Electronic Computer Manufacturing accounts for $38.7M. Remaining NAICS markets range from $216K down to $0. This profile covers contract obligations; largest-awards and expiring-contracts tables report total contract values rather than obligations. Current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date, and the page is labeled Countertrade Products: Federal Contracts, Agencies & Awards.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did Countertrade Products perform compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Countertrade Products obligated $244M in FY26, down from $492M in FY25 and from earlier peaks such as $390M in FY23. Obligations fell 50.5% year over year. The five-year series shows FY21 at $230M, FY22 at $340M, FY23 at $390M, FY24 at $322M, and FY25 at $492M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the top buyers and what markets drive obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $106M and is the top buying agency for Countertrade Products, followed by the Department of the Interior at $39.3M and the Department of Homeland Security at $20.3M. Other Computer Related Services is the largest market at $204M, with Electronic Computer Manufacturing at $38.7M and smaller markets ranging from $216K to $0."
      }
    ]
  },
